  "slug": "protein-golgi-1670",
  "summary": "Three preprints pair CRISPR perturbation or enhancer-network analysis with differentiation models to resolve dynamic, condition-specific regulatory architecture.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is resolving how regulatory architecture changes across differentiation and perturbation, using single-cell, spatial or chromatin-contact readouts. The cardioid study applies a time-resolved CRISPR screen of 400 CHD genes in daily-sampled cardioids (~975k cells) with a multiome atlas to expose stage-dependent regulatory logic. The Hi-C network analysis spans three differentiation systems and finds that super-enhancers rewire more than other elements and link to identity genes. Perturb-Tags extends screening to spatial multi-omics in embryoid bodies, capturing non-autonomous responses, and uses a GNN to model perturbation effects. None of these items addresses subcellular proteome organization directly. What is new is the temporal and spatial resolution of perturbation effects, moving from static regulator lists to context- and stage-dependent wiring.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
